TESTDATA["fce5-t3"] = {"title":"Cambridge FCE 5 · Test 3","format":"2008","reading":[{"part":1,"title":"","text":"A light breeze ruffled the steel-blue ocean. Roy Mason looked out over the beach, hands curled round his second mug of tea of the day. The first had been just before he left his tiny stone cottage high up in one of the winding streets that made up the village of Everdene. If he could have had a pound for every time someone stuck a note through his door asking if he wanted to sell, he would have been able to afford one of the posh new homes that were being built on the top road, had he wanted one. The developer's sign proudly boasted that nearly all of them had been sold. There might be a problem selling houses in the rest of the country, but not here. Not when the air smelled sweet, the surrounding hills were soft and rolling and the view took your breath away. Roy wouldn’t tire of it. Not that he’d ever seen much else. The writing on his mug might say that he loved New York, but he’d never been. His sister had brought it back when she'd been on a shopping trip there. Roy was happy that she’d enjoyed the experience but he failed to see what all the fuss was about. He drained the last of the sugary tea, put down the mug and collected up his tools — proper tools, with wooden handles that had moulded themselves to fit his hands over time, smooth and solid beneath his fingers, not like the lightweight plastic ones they sold that snapped and bent as soon as you put them to use. To Roy’s mind, there was no point in doing something unless you gave it your best. He never cut corners. He did things properly. The same couldn’t be said of everyone. For instance, someone last year put an advertisement in the local village shop undercutting his prices, and a few of the chalet owners had been tempted. The guy might have been cheap but he was clumsy and inexperienced. Roy had watched him attempting to hang a new door. It was comical. He felt sorry for him. He was only trying to make a living, but he hadn’t a clue. In the end, he’d given up, gone off and Roy had picked up with the old customers where he’d left off, no hard feelings, nothing said. He wasn’t one to bear a grudge. Roy’s father had built the wooden beach chalets and Roy was the unofficial caretaker, doing maintenance and repairs. Some owners were mean and only paid for their chalet to be repaired once every three or four years. A false economy. The wind and rain that swept through over the winter was unforgiving; the wood needed protection. Some owners kept their chalets plain; others saw it as an excuse to express their personality and chose garish colours that somehow worked in the seaside setting, a fairground riot of reds and greens and pinks and oranges. Some of them had their own names. Nothing sparklingly original, but it added to the sense that this was a community, that each chalet was a home from home. He usually spent the morning working on the chalets, At night he took people fishing for sea bass. It satisfied an inner longing in them, particularly younger men looking for new experiences. There was something about fishing that appealed to them and Roy found their enthusiasm infectious. Older people rarely took to it in the same way — they were always slightly mystified by the attraction, if they ever ventured out. He could see they were cold and bored. They would much rather buy their sea bass from an old van that drove along the coast road every evening. His mobile rang in the pocket of his shorts; another reason for the popularity of this beach - a good phone signal for all those career people who needed to keep in touch with the office. Roy didn’t see the point. It wasn’t a holiday if your employers could check up on you or if you could check up on them. But that seemed to be the way of the world.","items":[{"n":1,"q":"In the first paragraph, what do we learn about Roy?","options":["He wanted to move to one of the new houses in the area.","He was worried that he might have to leave his house.","He knew a lot of people were keen to buy his house.","He felt that his house was too small for him."],"answer":"C"},{"n":2,"q":"How did Roy feel about his village?","options":["He missed it when he left on shopping trips.","He didn’t want to live anywhere else.","A trip to New York had made him like it even more.","He was sad that others didn’t share his affection for it."],"answer":"B"},{"n":3,"q":"What does ‘cut corners’ in line 31 mean?","options":["do something that is illegal","do something that is dangerous","do something that is unnecessary","do something without sufficient care"],"answer":"D"},{"n":4,"q":"How did Roy react to the arrival of a competitor?","options":["He was worried that he would no longer be able to make a decent wage.","He was sympathetic to someone wanting to earn money.","He was surprised that the guy’s prices were so different.","He was angry that he had lost some of his customers."],"answer":"B"},{"n":5,"q":"What disappointed Roy about some chalet owners?","options":["They didn’t pay enough attention to the condition of their chalets.","They gave their chalets strange and unsuitable names.","They painted their chalets in very bright colours.","They didn’t always pay him regularly."],"answer":"A"},{"n":6,"q":"What does ‘it’ in line 56 refer to?","options":["owning a chalet","lacking originality","using garish colours","giving chalets their own names"],"answer":"D"},{"n":7,"q":"What was Roy’s attitude to the people he took fishing?","options":["He shared the excitement of the younger ones.","He was surprised by the reaction of the older ones.","He was irritated by those who decided to buy sea bass.","He hoped to persuade more of them to enjoy the experience."],"answer":"A"},{"n":8,"q":"How did Roy feel about mobile phones?","options":["He was surprised to get a signal on the beach.","He didn’t enjoy carrying one when he was at work.","He was annoyed by the number that he heard.","He failed to understand why visitors relied on them so much."],"answer":"D"}]},{"part":2,"title":"A catalogue of sporting memories","text":"Imagine that you are attending a Cup Final in which a relatively unsuccessful football team enjoys an unexpected triumph. Whatever you do, you should make absolutely certain that you don’t throw away the programme that you bought on your way into the ground to tell you about the game, because it, and other sports-related objects, might be worth a lot of money one day. Auction houses and dealers have quickly realised the potential in such nostalgia and are now exploiting it to attract a new audience. [9] Football, the national British sport, is perfect for this as it is rich in such treasures. [10] But it is only in recent years that auction houses have entered the market, bringing national exposure and helping prices to rise dramatically. Mike Ashton, a specialist in sporting memorabilia, says, ‘It is a growing market. I’ve seen prices rising extremely quickly. Most of the people bidding are private collectors and dealers; however, there are also some investors at the auctions.’ Ashton goes on, ‘There is plenty of material of unbelievable quality and quantity hidden away and forgotten in private houses all over this country. [11] His favourite story is of an old lady who was clearing out her attic and found her husband’s old autograph collection. Ashton says, ‘She was literally set to throw it in the bin. But her son told her to wait and called us.’ After examining it, Ashton telephoned her and asked her to sit down. When he told her it was worth more than £10,000, she was amazed. [12] The recent growth in football’s popularity in the UK has been a large factor in the increasing demand for memorabilia over the last few years. [13] An example of this is the growing number of football museums, as well as themed restaurants, which have a constant need for exhibits. All of this has helped to push up the value of football nostalgia. [14] For example, a beginner looking to invest in nostalgia can pick up programmes from the 1950s for just £5. According to experts, it is not Cup Finals and pre-1940s photos that make the best investment. [15] These are bound to increase in value. To summarise: be focused, try to stick to quality and enjoy owning a small bit of football history which might well be worth a lot one day.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"In fact, the signatures, dating back more than 60 years, fetched nearly twice that amount at auction."},{"letter":"B","text":"Instead, their advice is that would-be collectors should begin by choosing a favourite team or player and start with programmes from the 1980s onwards, which can be bought cheaply."},{"letter":"C","text":"However, it is clear that there are still some reasonably cheap deals to be found."},{"letter":"D","text":"This period has seen football gradually become a more business-like entertainment with greater appeal."},{"letter":"E","text":"This is not a new fashion, as private collectors have been storing programmes, autographs and annuals for decades."},{"letter":"F","text":"It was very interesting to study the writing and layout of football programmes and the development of the adverts in them."},{"letter":"G","text":"I receive calls on a daily basis from people who find what they refer to as “old rubbish” in their lofts and wonder if it is worth anything."},{"letter":"H","text":"Their aim is to make people realise that items connected to a particular sporting event can provide an excellent investment opportunity."}],"answers":{"9":"H","10":"E","11":"G","12":"A","13":"D","14":"C","15":"B"}},{"part":3,"title":"My greatest ever sea voyage","stem":"Which sailor mentions the following?","sections":[{"letter":"A","title":"Elaine","text":"On 1st June 1995, at the age of 18, I set off aboard Adelphi, my tiny 7-metre boat, to circle the British Isles. The media, my family and friends lined the dock sides to bid farewell as I slipped out of the gates of the marina. I was to spend five and a half months without seeing anyone I knew. Since then I’ve sailed all over the world. As a result, I’ve done things, like learning French and visiting other countries, which I’d never have done otherwise. At one time I had to put my own money aside for the Adelphi’s new sails but things are different now. I’m sponsored by a large international firm, and with their help, I’m building a 20-metre race boat."},{"letter":"B","title":"Barbara","text":"After years of being weekend sailors, my husband and I decided to sell our home and sail round the world. Now we've started our voyage across the Pacific. At the beginning, New Zealand, our destination, was seven months away. In between was the vastness of the Pacific, speckled with dozens of tiny islands. Part of the enjoyment of cruising is in spending time away from the world when only our skills count. We’ve been exploring some enchanting islands, believing each one is the best — until the next one! At the moment we’re two-thirds through the trip, but the thrill increases as the end draws near."},{"letter":"C","title":"Robert","text":"In the Sail Training Association, we operate two sailing ships which take young people from a variety of backgrounds for a two-week ‘tall ships’ experience. It isn’t designed to create expert sailors, although some sailing knowledge does get absorbed. It’s more a character-developing activity which increases their self-esteem. My most memorable voyage on one of these ships took us to the west coast of Ireland. The weather was awful, with gale force winds and driving rain that the Irish seamen call a light mist! The youngsters soon settled down together, overcoming the misery of seasickness, while accepting the fixed schedule of the watchkeeping system that kept them on deck for a daily minimum of four hours. By the time we sailed for home, they'd become a capable crew, and I remember feeling how good it was to see the pure enjoyment on the trainees’ faces."},{"letter":"D","title":"Michel","text":"It was the 1998 Globe Challenge — the non-stop, round-the-world race that I’d dreamed of ever since first going yachting as a child. Everything had gone according to plan until suddenly I was experiencing every sailor’s worst nightmare. In the mountainous seas of the Southern Ocean, a giant wave hit the yacht, and as I was flung across the deck, hurting myself in the process and breaking my collar bone, I knew my boat was damaged. I was in the middle of nowhere. My yacht was sinking and I became increasingly ill. In fact, by the following day I knew I couldn’t survive much longer. But then Matt Evans, a fellow competitor, emerged on the scene, having heard my desperate radio pleas for help. He had sailed 250 kilometres right back into the heart of the storm to save me."},{"letter":"E","title":"Steve","text":"One of my most challenging races was round Britain’s most northerly headland. Even in July it’s cold when the wind blows from the north-west, and blow it most certainly did! My companion and I were determined to win the race. The wind was extremely strong, and the boat took us for the ride of a lifetime, surfing down the backs of the huge waves. During our regular spell of two hours on watch, each of us wanted to top the other’s best speed, and the stimulus of that friendly rivalry lent a delicious excitement to the race. The advantage of having two of you is that even when there’s work to do on deck, it doesn’t stop the curry being cooked down below. We arrived, all smiles, at our destination, and the opposition didn’t arrive till later the next day, which gave us a further reason to grin."}],"items":[{"n":16,"q":"being dependent on their sailing ability","answer":"B"},{"n":17,"q":"the continuing delights of new places","answer":"B"},{"n":18,"q":"pleasure in victory","answer":"E"},{"n":19,"q":"fulfilling a life-long ambition","answer":"D"},{"n":20,"q":"receiving an injury during bad weather","answer":"D"},{"n":21,"q":"being involved in a confidence-building project","answer":"C"},{"n":22,"q":"saving up in order to purchase sailing equipment","answer":"A"},{"n":23,"q":"an example of selfless behaviour","answer":"D"},{"n":24,"q":"a voyage yet to be completed","answer":"B"},{"n":25,"q":"the need to perform domestic tasks on board","answer":"E"},{"n":26,"q":"the opportunity to develop skills unrelated to sailing","answer":"A"},{"n":27,"q":"some of the negative aspects of the normal sailor routine","answer":"C"},{"n":28,"q":"a lack of companionship","answer":"A"},{"n":29,"q":"the competitive atmosphere amongst the crew","answer":"E"},{"n":30,"q":"gaining satisfaction from the achievements of others","answer":"C"}]}],"writing":[{"part":1,"items":[{"n":1,"kind":"letter","task":"You must answer this question. Write your answer in 120–150 words in an appropriate style.\n\nYou have invited Paul Cox, an English cyclist who has won many races, to give a talk to your English class. Read Paul’s letter and the notes you have made. Then write a letter to Paul, using all your notes.\n\nI’d be delighted to come and talk to your English class.\nIt’s always good to know something about the people I’m talking to, so could you tell me some information about the students in your English class?\nI can give a talk either on ‘My life as a champion cyclist’ or on ‘Bicycles – the transport of the future’. Which talk would be better?\nWhile I’m in your area I’d like to do some sightseeing. Could you give me some ideas about where I could go?\nKind regards,\nPaul\n\nNotes:\n• Thank Paul\n• Explain\n• Tell Paul\n• Suggest …\n\nWrite your letter.\n\nYou must use grammatically correct sentences with accurate spelling and punctuation in a style appropriate for the situation.","img":"data:image/png;base64,","points":["ringraziare Paul","dare informazioni sugli studenti della classe","dire a Paul quale conferenza sarebbe meglio e perché","suggerire dove fare un giro turistico"]}]},{"part":2,"items":[{"n":2,"kind":"article","task":"Write your answer in 120–180 words in an appropriate style.\n\nYou see this announcement in an international magazine. We are publishing a series of articles about markets around the world. Tell us about your favourite market, what you can buy there and what you like about it. We will publish the most interesting articles in next month’s magazine. Write your article.","img":"","points":["descrivere il tuo mercato preferito","dire cosa si può comprare","dire cosa ti piace del mercato"]},{"n":3,"kind":"essay","task":"Write your answer in 120–180 words in an appropriate style.\n\nYou have had a class discussion about fashion. Now your English teacher has asked you to write an essay discussing the following statement: The clothes you wear show a lot about your personality. What do the clothes people wear show about their personalities? Write your essay.","img":"","points":["discutere che cosa mostrano i vestiti sulla personalità di chi li indossa"]},{"n":4,"kind":"report","task":"Write your answer in 120–180 words in an appropriate style.\n\nRecently there have been some complaints from students about your college cafe. Your College Principal has asked you to write a report about the cafe and make recommendations on how to improve:\n• the food and drink available\n• the furniture and decoration\n• the entertainment facilities\n\nWrite your report.","img":"","points":["cibo e bevande","arredamento e decorazione","intrattenimento","raccomandazioni per migliorare il bar"]}]}],"useofenglish":[{"part":1,"title":"The importance of drawing","text":"In the nineteenth century, John Ruskin, an English writer and art critic, made great efforts to (0) ……… (encourage) people to draw. He believed that drawing was a skill that was greatly neglected in schools and [1] that it was more important to the human [2] than writing. In order to do something to improve the [3] he published two books on drawing and gave a series of lectures at the Working Men’s College in London. His books were [4] read, and his lectures [5] large audiences. This further [6] Ruskin’s belief that everybody should be given the opportunity to learn how to draw. Ruskin’s efforts were not [7] at turning people into good artists but at making them happier. For him, drawings were of value even when they were done by people with no talent, as drawing teaches people to [8] things rather than just to see them. He felt that when we are involved in the process of drawing something, we have to look at it very [9] and become aware of the different parts which [10] up the whole. It is in this way that we [11] to a deeper appreciation and [12] of the thing itself.","gaps":{"1":{"options":["claimed","convinced","recommended","expressed"],"answer":"A"},"2":{"options":["family","people","race","beings"],"answer":"C"},"3":{"options":["state","condition","case","situation"],"answer":"D"},"4":{"options":["vastly","broadly","immensely","widely"],"answer":"D"},"5":{"options":["gained","collected","attracted","brought"],"answer":"C"},"6":{"options":["strengthened","raised","ensured","grew"],"answer":"A"},"7":{"options":["designed","aimed","pointed","intended"],"answer":"B"},"8":{"options":["observe","regard","witness","look"],"answer":"A"},"9":{"options":["closely","distinctly","definitely","exactly"],"answer":"A"},"10":{"options":["work","set","make","take"],"answer":"C"},"11":{"options":["arrive","come","reach","achieve"],"answer":"B"},"12":{"options":["meaning","intelligence","understanding","significance"],"answer":"C"}}},{"part":2,"title":"A new kind of pollution","text":"Air pollution is a well-known problem, but now light pollution (0) ……… (is) also causing concern. Light pollution occurs because there are many electric lights around us which are so powerful that [13] stop Us seeing the stars in the night sky. It is now [14] a problem that in some places people [15] outdoor lighting is too bright could even [16] given a heavy fine. Business owners, supermarkets and even home-owners [17] install security lights may face legal action if their lights are [18] bright that they spoil their neighbours’ enjoyment of the night sky. Amazingly, many children growing up in big cities today [19] never seen the night sky properly because of the increase in light pollution. And it is [20] only in cities - people living in the country are also affected [21] lights from nearby towns. It is a problem for those simply trying to enjoy the stars, and for serious astronomers [22] well. One university in the UK has come up [23] an interesting solution. It has built a telescope in the Canary Islands and allows schools to use this via the internet. But it does seem ridiculous that children can’t see the stars properly [24] using their computers!","answers":{"13":["they"],"14":["such"],"15":["whose"],"16":["be / get"],"17":["who / that"],"18":["so"],"19":["have"],"20":["not"],"21":["by"],"22":["as"],"23":["with"],"24":["without"]}},{"part":3,"title":"Looking into the past","text":"Some years ago, plans to build a shopping centre on a piece of land in Glasgow were delayed when photographs taken from the air showed that there were marks of ancient houses that had once stood on this land. Archaeologists found the photographs (0) ……… (impressive) and were keen to carry out an [25] on the land. They knew that once the shopping centre was built, the archaeological [26] would be hidden forever. [27], building work was due to start just a few days after the [28] of the houses was made. The archaeologists wanted to delay the start of building work and held urgent [29] with the [30] to request that the building work should not begin immediately. An agreement was reached and the archaeologists were given eleven weeks to find out more about the houses before building work began. They found that the houses had been made of wood and were circular in shape. They had [31] been surrounded by a deep ditch. What remained of the [32] was some holes in the ground where the [33] of the houses had built a fence some two metres in height, presumably to offer them some [34] from attack.","stems":{"25":"INVESTIGATE","26":"EVIDENT","27":"FORTUNATE","28":"DISCOVER","29":"DISCUSS","30":"DEVELOP","31":"ORIGIN","32":"SETTLE","33":"INHABIT","34":"PROTECT"},"answers":{"25":["investigation"],"26":["evidence"],"27":["unfortunately"],"28":["discovery"],"29":["discussions"],"30":["developer / developers"],"31":["originally"],"32":["settlement / settlements"],"33":["inhabitants"],"34":["protection"]}},{"part":4,"items":[{"n":35,"first":"“I’m not sure I was right about the date of Susanna’s birthday,” said Ann.","key":"MISTAKE","before":"“I may","after":"about the date of Susanna’s birthday,” said Ann.","answers":["have made | a mistake"]},{"n":36,"first":"It is too late to go to the football match now.","key":"POINT","before":"There is","after":"to the football match now.","answers":["little point / no point | (in) going"]},{"n":37,"first":"Ramesh said, “I’ll let you borrow my guitar, if you promise to take care of it.”","key":"LONG","before":"Ramesh said I could borrow his guitar","after":"to take care of it.","answers":["so long as / as long as | I promise / I promised"]},{"n":38,"first":"The popularity of snowboarding has grown considerably in recent years.","key":"MUCH","before":"Snowboarding","after":"now than it used to be.","answers":["is much | more popular"]},{"n":39,"first":"Very wisely, my grandfather has decided not to drive any more.","key":"GIVE","before":"My grandfather’s decision","after":"is very wise.","answers":["to give up | driving"]},{"n":40,"first":"The guests at the party ate very little food.","key":"WAS","before":"Hardly","after":"by the guests at the party.","answers":["any food / anything | was eaten"]},{"n":41,"first":"I didn’t take any notice of George’s behaviour.","key":"ATTENTION","before":"I didn’t pay","after":"George was behaving.","answers":["(any) attention to | how / the way"]},{"n":42,"first":"Martin still finds it strange to speak English every day.","key":"USED","before":"Martin still has not","after":"English every day.","answers":["become used / grown used / got used | to speaking / to using"]}]}],"listening":[{"part":1,"audio":"audio/fce5-t3-l1.mp3","items":[{"n":1,"q":"You hear a man speaking on the radio about scientists. What is his attitude to scientists?","options":["He doesn’t always trust them.","He respects their opinions.","He regrets not having their ability."],"answer":"A"},{"n":2,"q":"You overhear two people talking on a bus. Where does the woman think she lost her mobile phone?","options":["inher car","inher office","inashop"],"answer":"C"},{"n":3,"q":"You turn on the radio and hear the beginning of a programme. What type of programme is being introduced?","options":["adocumentary about the environment","afeature about holidays","apersonal story about a journey"],"answer":"B"},{"n":4,"q":"You hear an artist talking about his schooldays. What made him interested in sculpture as a career?","options":["His teacher showed him new sculpture techniques.","His family believed he had a talent for sculpture.","His first attempt at sculpture was highly praised."],"answer":"C"},{"n":5,"q":"You overhear two students talking. What do they agree about?","options":["It’s important to study.","It’s necessary to find a job.","It’s an appropriate time to travel."],"answer":"C"},{"n":6,"q":"You overhear a swimming instructor talking to a learner about his swimming technique. What mistake has the learner just made?","options":["He forgot the correct hand position.","He moved his arms too soon.","Hekicked too weakly."],"answer":"B"},{"n":7,"q":"On the radio, you hear a fashion designer talking about her life. What was she like as a child?","options":["very strong-minded","highly competitive","very dependent on her parents"],"answer":"A"},{"n":8,"q":"You overhear a woman leaving a message on an answering machine. Why is she phoning?","options":["torearrange a meeting","to apologise for something she’s done","toask a favour"],"answer":"C"}]},{"part":2,"intro":"You will hear part of an interview with a man called Damon Walker, who works as the manager of a wildlife park in the north of England. For questions 9–18, complete the sentences.","audio":"audio/fce5-t3-l2.mp3","notes":"The wildlife park\nThe wildlife park was originally a [9] when Damon’s father bought it.\nA year after the wildlife park was opened, Damon’s father brought in different kinds of [10] and birds.\nAs a child, Damon says his favourite animals were the [11] in the wildlife park.\nDamon chose to become a [12] after he left school.\nDamon’s experience with [13] in Australia made him decide to work in the wildlife park.\nDamon says that when running a wildlife park, the [14] are the most difficult aspect of the job.\nAccording to Damon, they receive between [15] job applications for the wildlife park each month.\nDamon gets satisfaction out of a recent [16] which he has started, to teach local schoolchildren about wildlife.\nDamon is planning to put in some [17] which will increase the security of the animals.\nDamon is constructing a [18] for children, using profits from the wildlife park.","answers":{"9":["farm"],"10":["fish / fishes"],"11":["camels"],"12":["diver"],"13":["(seeing) dolphins"],"14":["(long) (working) hours / (long) hours (of work)"],"15":["10 (and) 20 / ten (and) twenty"],"16":["(wildlife) project (with local schools)"],"17":["(video) cameras"],"18":["playground"]}},{"part":3,"intro":"You will hear five different musicians talking about writing songs. For questions 19–23, choose from the list (A–F) what each speaker says. Use the letters only once. There is one extra letter which you do not need to use.","audio":"audio/fce5-t3-l3.mp3","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The best songs are written quite easily."},{"letter":"B","text":"I could not write songs with other people."},{"letter":"C","text":"My best song was not written in my usual way."},{"letter":"D","text":"One song owed its success to being changed in the studio."},{"letter":"E","text":"All songwriters have different approaches to songwriting."},{"letter":"F","text":"It is easier to write only the music to a song."}],"items":[{"n":19,"label":"Speaker 1","answer":"D"},{"n":20,"label":"Speaker 2","answer":"B"},{"n":21,"label":"Speaker 3","answer":"C"},{"n":22,"label":"Speaker 4","answer":"F"},{"n":23,"label":"Speaker 5","answer":"A"}]},{"part":4,"intro":"You will hear an interview with Sheila McLennan, whose daughter Liz takes part in gymnastics competitions. For questions 24–30, choose the best answer (A, B or C).","audio":"audio/fce5-t3-l4.mp3","items":[{"n":24,"q":"Sheila says that before important competitions,","options":["her daughter is able to cope with the pressure well.","she herself feels too anxious to watch.","her husband is considerably more nervous than she is."],"answer":"A"},{"n":25,"q":"How does Sheila feel about Liz’s achievements?","options":["proud to be involved in Liz’s training","pleased to watch Liz compete abroad","sorry that Liz no longer lives at home"],"answer":"B"},{"n":26,"q":"Sheila says that when Liz was seven years old,","options":["Liz was unwilling to try new physical activities.","Liz liked one physical activity which was unsuitable.","Liz couldn’t find a physical activity she enjoyed."],"answer":"B"},{"n":27,"q":"Sheila says that now Liz doesn’t have much difficulty in","options":["relaxing.","keeping fit.","making friends."],"answer":"C"},{"n":28,"q":"In order to take Liz to her training sessions, Sheila has had to","options":["give up her own sport.","resign from her job.","buy asecond car."],"answer":"A"},{"n":29,"q":"Sheila thinks that Liz will eventually","options":["work full-time for a charity.","become a sports trainer.","find a career outside gymnastics."],"answer":"B"},{"n":30,"q":"Sheila thinks that Liz has been successful because of","options":["her physical skills.","her ability to learn.","her mental discipline."],"answer":"C"}]}]};
